{
  "raw": {
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Milwaukee/job/Milwaukee-WI/Criminal-Justice---Criminology-Open-Rank-Faculty_JR10016118": {
      "title": "Criminal Justice & Criminology Open Rank Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Criminal Justice & Criminology Open Rank Faculty Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Professor Job Duties: The Department of Criminal Justice & Criminology at the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee is seeking to fill a full-time tenured or tenure-track, open rank faculty position. Areas of specialization are open. Duties include advancing a robust program of research while maintaining a strong commitment to teaching and mentorship along with service to the department, university, profession, and community. The standard teaching load is 4 courses per year (2 courses per semester). The anticipated start date for the 9-month annual appointment is August 23rd, 2027. Key Job Responsibilities: Department: The University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee is a Carnegie-designated R1 institution and recognized by the Carnegie Elective Classification for Community Engagement. The Helen Bader School of Social Welfare is comprised of the Department of Criminal Justice & Criminology and the Department of Social Work. The Department of Criminal Justice & Criminology offers a BS and an MS, as well as jointly offering a PhD in Social Welfare with the Department of Social Work. The Department is known for its commitment to collaborative partnerships with community and governmental organizations in southeast Wisconsin and beyond through research and student engagement. Compensation: Minimum of $80,000/9-month basis, contingent on rank and qualifications as it relates to the position. Required Qualifications: Qualified applicants must hold a PhD/Doctorate in criminal justice, criminology, or a related field. Applicants who are ABD will be considered if completion of the degree is imminent (received on or before the start date of the position). Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated ability to produce significant research. Established record to secure and maintain extramural funding. Demonstrated ability to successfully teach undergraduate and graduate courses – both in person and online. Demonstrated ability to collaborate across disciplines and with community partners. Ability to meet rank of a tenured associate or full professor in the Department of Criminal Justice and Criminology at UWM which includes a strong record of research and scholarship, teaching, and service accomplishments. How to Apply: Applicants are required to apply online via Workday. A completed application will include: 1) a cover letter 2) curriculum vitae 3) research statement 4) teaching philosophy 5) contact information for three professional references Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach all required documents prior to submitting the online application to ensure full consideration. Additional documents submitted aside from these required materials will not be considered.  In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates’ application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications. This is a continuous recruitment with an initial review date of December 1, 2026. To ensure consideration, applications must be submitted by November 30, 2026. Application materials received after November 30, 2026 may not receive consideration. Contact Information: For any questions regarding the position, please contact Tom Schwartz (schwa282@uwm.edu). Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Milwaukee/job/Milwaukee-WI/Education-Administration-and-Supervision-Teaching-Faculty_JR10015797": {
      "title": "Education Administration and Supervision Teaching Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Education Administration and Supervision Teaching Faculty Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Teaching Faculty III Job Duties: The Educational Administration and Supervision program is housed in the Department of Educational Psychology and Leadership. The program prepares individuals for many careers in school, district, network, and other organizational leadership and administration through a master’s program and DPI-approved administrative licensure programs. Specializations lead to single or dual licensure as Principal (5051), Director of Instruction (5010), and/or Director of Special Education and Pupil Services (5080).  We are hiring a Core Teaching Faculty position to help lead the Educational Administration and Supervision program. This is a 9-month position with potential for summer teaching as available. The successful candidate will (1) teach graduate level courses on education administration, (2) provide administrative service for the program, and 3) serve as a core faculty member and attend department and program-related functions (e.g., program faculty meetings, etc.). We are particularly interested in candidates with experience in K-12 educational administration, and experience (or interest) in college-level instruction. The program has a strong commitment to culturally informed and equitable pedagogy and training. For more information, please visit the program’s web page:  https://uwm.edu/education/departments/administrative-leadership/   Key Job Responsibilities: Program coordination, including managing student recruitment, admissions processes, retention, practica and capstone activities, and degree and licensure approval for three licensure programs (#5010, #5051, #5080).   Teaching courses in the area of Educational Administration and developing curricular innovations as needed.  Serve as the administrative licensure program area liaison with the Wisconsin Department of Public Instruction (DPI), and the UWM School of Education Council for Professional Education (CPE).  Coordinate with SOE personnel on maintaining programmatic records and develop required reports for the University and the Wisconsin Department of Public Instruction.  Engagement in the Department of Educational Psychology & Leadership and the School of Education. Department: The UWM School of Education (SOE) offers undergraduate, masters, and doctoral degree programs, and certifications in over 46 program areas. The SOE faculty recognizes the necessity to be leaders for equity and social justice to improve our nation’s public educational system The School’s key mission is to provide leadership and inspiration for learning and human development in urban communities, and its central vision is to become a premier urban school of education recognized for its diversity and known for excellence in teaching, learning and research. As a community of scholars, the SOE faculty views research as a primary asset since effective educational and societal decisions are derived from that knowledge-base. This asset affirms the faculty’s intent to continue to adapt, develop, and expand its efforts to serve diverse populations, train future scholars and practitioners, and promote an atmosphere of free inquiry. Please visit the school’s website at www.uwm.edu/education   Compensation: The target hiring salary is $60,000-$72,000/annually on an academic, 9-month basis.  Final salary offer will be contingent upon degree and the knowledge, skills, and abilities demonstrated throughout the selection process.  Required Qualifications: Hold an MS, MA, or MSED or higher degree in Educational Administration, Administrative Leadership, or a closely-related field from an accredited higher education institution by the time of application.   Ability to teach college-level courses in the field of educational administration, including familiarity with high impact practices for teaching online courses.  Prior K-12 teaching and/or administrative experience in K-12 settings. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in program administration and coordination, preferably in an educational administration (K-12) setting. Familiarity with DPI (Department of Public Instruction) requirements for administrative licenses such as principal, director of instruction, and director of special education and pupil services in the state of Wisconsin Hold an Ed.D. or Ph.D. in Educational Administration, Administrative Leadership, or a closely-related field from an accredited higher education institution by the time of application.  Experience working with diverse student populations and demonstrated commitment to equity and inclusion through teaching, research, and/or service. How to Apply: Interested applicants are required to apply online a provide a cover letter and detailed CV/resume that addresses your professional level work experience as it relates to all required and preferred qualifications.  PLEASE NOTE: cover letter and CV/resume will need to be uploaded to the same upload area in the application.     Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach all required documents prior to submitting the online application to ensure full consideration. Additional documents submitted aside from a cover letter and CV /resume will not be considered. Finalist candidate(s) being considered for hire will be required to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified. In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates’ application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications Apply by the application deadline, October 30, 2026. Questions about the position should be directed to Josh Xiong (jxxiong@uwm.edu). Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Milwaukee/job/Milwaukee-WI/Master-Teacher_JR10012243": {
      "title": "Master Teacher (part time)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Master Teacher (part time) Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Terminal (Fixed Term) Job Profile: Professor of Practice Job Duties: The Master Instructors are responsible for direct student assessment and instruction, progress documentation, academic progress, family communication, and attendance and participation at all professional development and presession planning seminars. Responsible for direct student assessment and instruction with fidelity to CLIFFTOP intervention model. The work is performed onsite at UWM's Childrens Learning Center in the evenings. Job Responsibilities 80%  – Master Instructors will assess students, analyze data and write assessment-based intervention plans based on the CLIFFTOP model, as well as draft initial and progress reports, which are shared with families. As the foundation for student instruction, these lesson plans will include semester objectives, teaching strategies, and home extension plans. Master Instructors deliver instruction under the direction of the Director over the course of the semester adjusting instruction based on ongoing monitoring of student progress. 15% – Communicate with families about scheduling, student reports, and weekly home extensions. 5% – Actively participate in initial and ongoing professional development. Key Job Responsibilities: Provides classroom, online, and/or laboratory instruction including grading Supervises the day to day activities of work unit employees involved in instructional support as needed Develops teaching techniques that enhance course effectiveness in alignment with desired outcomes and established strategy Assists in developing techniques and/or model programs for carrying out instructional goals in disciplinary area and may help identify ways in which new techniques could be of assistance Assesses learner performance and prepares reports recommending instructional improvements Assists in defining the objectives of the program and play a major role in carrying out program duties Department: The  Stritch Family Literacy Center combines decades of university-led literacy research with compassionate, individualized instruction to help every child reach their full reading potential. Our collaborative approach—connecting families, instructors, and schools—ensures that each child gains the confidence, skills, and love of reading that open doors for a lifetime. Compensation: Tutoring: Lump sum pay rate of $1400/per student/per semester  Pre-tutoring assessment/Pre- and Post-tutoring reporting: $350 per student/semester -This is a part time position. The number of students assigned to each Master Teacher is determined by need of the SFLC and availability of the selected Master Teacher. Total: $1750 pay per student/semester Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in related field Holds relevant Wisconsin Teaching Certification or equivalent Working knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite Preferred Qualifications: Classroom literacy or intervention experience Holds relevant Wisconsin Teaching Certification: reading teacher license (1316) and/or reading specialist license (5017) Master of Arts or Masters of Science in Reading/Literacy, Special Education, or Speech and Language How to Apply: Interested applicants are required to apply online and must submit all of the following documents at the time of application to be fully considered: Letter of application addressing the job requirements Curriculum vitae Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach all required documents prior to submitting the online application to ensure full consideration. Additional documents submitted aside from the documents listed above will not be considered. The next positions hired will begin tutoring in summer or fall 2027 depending on need. This is a continuous recruitment with a next review date of February 1, 2027. Applications submitted after January 31, 2027 may not be reviewed.   In instances where the Search and Screen is unable to ascertain from the candidates' application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications. Contact Information: Questions about this position should be directed to Kristine Lize at kcmarver@uwm.edu. Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Milwaukee/job/Milwaukee-WI/Information-Law-and-Ethics-Teaching-Faculty-_JR10015388": {
      "title": "Information Law and Ethics Teaching Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Information Law and Ethics Teaching Faculty  Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Teaching Faculty II Job Duties: The  School of Information Studies (SOIS) , the iSchool at the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee (UWM), invites applications for a Teaching Faculty position in  Information Law and Ethics . This is a full-time, 9-month, renewable, instructional academic staff position ( Teaching Faculty II ) based on the main campus for both face-to-face and fully online methods of delivery at the Bachelor’s and Master’s levels.  This position requires a commitment to excellence in teaching and student success as well as a commitment to continuous growth and professional development to keep abreast of the legal and ethical issues in libraries, industry, information professions, and higher education trends affecting the curriculum.   The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to a positive work environment in the school and college and to participate in school and college-wide initiatives and events.  The desired start date for the position is January 8, 2027 . Key Job Responsibilities: Facilitate classroom and online instruction for assigned courses, including assessment of student performance.  Serve as an initial point of contact for students as it relates to specific course or series content and expectations.  Develop instructional design and curriculum relevant to a course of instruction.  College/School: The School of Information Studies (SOIS) offers several educational programs: a Bachelor of Science in Information Science and Technology (BSIST); a Masters of Science in Information Science and Technology (MSIST) degree in partnership with the Department of Computer Science; an ALA-accredited Masters of Library and Information Science (MLIS); several coordinated graduate degree programs, including Health Informatics; transcript-designated concentrations within the MLIS Archival Studies, Public Libraries, Information Organization, and Information Technology; post-masters Certificates of Advanced Study in Archives and Records Management and Digital Libraries; and a PhD in Information Studies. The School has a strong research faculty, more than 900 students, and state-of-the-art information technology.    SOIS is committed to excellence through diversity of thought, race, gender and community. We seek candidates who value and demonstrate an appreciation for diversity in all forms, in addition to providing the necessary support to those communities for an inclusive learning arena.  Required Qualifications:   Master’s degree, or higher, in library science, information science, law, or a closely related field from an accredited US institution or international equivalent by the start date of the appointment.  Knowledge and expertise in the legal and ethical issues in librarianship and/or information technology.  Evidence of university-level teaching experience.  Preferred Qualifications:   Terminal degree (i.e. PhD or JD) in information science, library science, law, or a closely related discipline.   Knowledge and experience in legal and ethical issues in the public and/or private sectors.  Demonstrated ability in the design and delivery of courses in face-to-face and online formats.  Professional experience in libraries, information organizations, legal practice related to information/IP, or technology policy.  Compensation: The target hiring range is $75,000-$85,000 on a 9-month, academic basis. Final salary will be contingent upon the knowledge, skills, and abilities demonstrated throughout the selection process.  Application Instructions:   Applicants are required to apply online. A strong application packet will include a cover letter highlighting their suitability for this position as well as knowledge and teaching experience in one or more of the areas required (including how minimum and preferred qualifications for this position are met), and a current CV.    Finalist candidate(s) being considered for hire may be required to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified.  Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach all required documents prior to submitting the online application to ensure full consideration. Additional documents submitted aside from a cover letter and CV will not be considered.    In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates’ application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications.   Apply by application deadline, 10/25/2026. Questions about the position should be directed to Trevor Maloney at maloney3@uwm.edu. Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Milwaukee/job/Milwaukee-WI/Clinic-Director_JR10015289": {
      "title": "Clinic Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nPart time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Clinic Director Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Terminal (Fixed Term) Job Profile: Clinical Professor Job Duties: The Clinic Director oversees all aspects of the functioning of the UWM Psychology Clinic, which is a research and training clinic serving UWM students and the Milwaukee community.  This position will work with faculty supervisors and students to develop, implement, and monitor compliance with clinic policies, manage the clinic budget in consultation with the Clinical Training Committee, provide clinical supervision for graduate students in the Clinical Psychology PhD program and supervise Psychotherapy teams each year.  The Clinic Director will serve as a voting member on the Clinical Training Committee, which oversees the clinical training of graduate students in the Clinical Psychology PhD program, and any other responsibilities which are more fully described in the position description.     Key Job Responsibilities: Assist in hiring clinic staff  Develop and oversee clinic budget allocations and expenditures  Oversee compliance with Clinic and University Policy and Legal/Ethical standards for Psychologists  Train & supervise clinic staff (e.g., Clinic Manager, Graduate Student Clinic TA)  Supervise front desk staff  Clinical student orientation to Clinic policies  Oversee electronic health records. Identify and manage appropriate software, train clinic personnel in use of software, ensure timely completion of records, safe storage of records, and ethical release of records when needed  Maintain manuals for clinical policies and procedures for students, staff, and supervisors  Supervise client intake assessments conducted by Graduate Student Clinic TA  Assign clients to therapist  Assist the DCT/Area Head in assigning students to supervisors  Engage in direct supervision of graduate students learning to conduct psychotherapy (supervise up to 6-8 student therapists in first year of therapy practicum, including holding regular group and individual supervision meetings and completing student evaluations), including continued supervision beyond the therapy year  Survey graduate students for feedback about clinic operations and hold periodic clinic operations meetings to review feedback to develop and/or discuss improvements in processes  Liaison to campus resources, Student Health and Wellness Center, Accessibility Resource Center, Women's Center, LGBTQ+ Resource Center  Serve as liaison between Clinical Psychology Program and Community Placement Centers  Oversee collection of practicum evaluations of Clinical Psychology students from both internal and external clinical supervisors  Liaise with clinic supervisors (both therapy and assessment) regarding clinic operations, infrastructure, and supervisory considerations  Oversee collection of Faculty supervisor evaluations by clinical psychology students  Coordinate clinical research - e.g., liaison with PIs for studies done through the clinic  Oversee implementation of measurement-based care  Meet with student therapists to discuss models of supervision and consultation {Psychology 842 syllabus)  Coordinate the Professional Developing and Issues in Clinical Psychology program meeting (approximately 3x/month)  Assist the DCT/Area Head in maintaining the records necessary for maintenance and renewal of APA accreditation  Keep abreast of the field in terms of Psychology Department Psychology Clinics  Teach 1 graduate course per year (e.g., Professional Issues and Ethics in Clinical Psychology)  Serve as a voting member of Clinical Training Committee  Department: The department of Psychological & Brain Sciences is among the largest departments on campus, and psychology is the largest undergraduate major program at UWM.   The neuroscience major was added in recent years to provide a streamlined curriculum for undergraduates that combines biology, biochemistry and psychology coursework.   We are home to highly productive researchers who garner larger public and private grant funding. Our research specialties are renown and center around three areas: clinical psychology, health psychology and neuroscience. Our excellent reputation for research, education, and training in psychology make our graduate program competitive nationally and internationally with many more applicants than seats available.   We pride ourselves on consistent efforts and attention to building a collegial atmosphere in the department with every student, staff and faculty member working towards our shared goals of creating and disseminating new knowledge in the fields of psychology and neuroscience. Compensation: $100,000 - $150,000 (Final salary offer will be determined based on qualifications and experience) Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Clinical Psychology, Counseling Psychology, or related field  Licensed or Licensed-eligible in WI  Demonstrated publication record  Demonstrated record of supervision of clinical psychology graduate students in empirically supported interventions and/or assessment practice  Demonstrated ability to work with clinical researchers  Demonstrated record of didactic teaching of graduate students in clinical psychology program or similar   Preferred Qualifications: ABPP in clinical, counseling, CBT, Health, or Neuropsychology  Familiarity with Clinic Note or similar electronic health record system  Record of budget management and other skills needed to oversee business operations of the clinic  How to Apply: Interested applicants are required to apply online and provide a cover letter, detailed resume/CV that addresses your professional level work experience as it relates to all required and preferred qualifications. Applications missing any of the required documents listed prior to the application deadline will be marked as incomplete and will not be considered. In instances where the Search and Screen Committee are unable to ascertain from a candidate’s application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they will be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications. Finalist candidate(s) being considered for hire will be required to provide three professional/supervisor references through an external reference checking tool. References will not be contacted prior to candidates being notified.   Apply electronically by the application deadline of Friday, October 23, 2026. Contact Information: For any questions regarding the position, please contact Thomas Schwartz (schwa282@uwm.edu). Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Milwaukee/job/Milwaukee-WI/Associate-Full-Professor-in-Social-Work--AI---Data-Science-Cluster_JR10015029-1": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor in Social Work, AI & Data Science Cluster",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Associate/Full Professor in Social Work, AI & Data Science Cluster Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Associate Professor Job Duties: The Social Work Department at the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee (UWM) invites applicants to apply to an associate or full professor, tenure-line faculty position as part of an Artificial Intelligence and Data Science Cluster Hire Initiative. UWM Academic Affairs, with generous support from the Northwestern Mutual Data Science Institute (NMDSI), is making a strategic investment in this position, providing competitive salary supplements and start-up resources.  The new faculty will have a record of securing external funding along with experience in academic settings that is commensurate with a rank of Advanced Assistant Professor (with ability to join UWM as tenured associate) or higher.  Applicants are expected to have expertise both in a critical social work-related area of research (e.g., applied gerontology, health disparities, poverty policy, child welfare, community practice, criminal justice, etc.) and in AI and Data Science. The incoming faculty will collaborate with NMDSI to drive innovative research, strengthen community engagement, and develop AI and Data Science talent to support Milwaukee’s growth as a technology and innovation hub. Candidates with relevant expertise will also have the opportunity to affiliate as Fellows with UWM’s Connected Systems, which houses the Microsoft AI Co-Innovation Lab      Faculty in this cluster hire will be housed in UWM’s College of Community Engagement & Professions: Helen Bader School of Social Welfare and the School of Information Studies.       The faculty hired for this cluster in the Department of Social Work will:  Produce scholarship in an important area of social work research.  Advance interdisciplinary AI and Data Science research at UWM.  Collaborate with NMDSI to address real-world challenges.  Innovate and expand AI and Data Science educational programs.  Key Job Responsibilities: Department: The Helen Bader School of Social Welfare is comprised of the Department of Social Work and the Department of Criminal Justice & Criminology. The Department of Criminal Justice & Criminology offers a BS and an MS, as well as jointly offering a PhD in Social Welfare with the Department of Social Work. The Department is known for its commitment to collaborative partnerships with community and governmental organizations in southeast Wisconsin and beyond through research and student engagement.  Why Join UWM? UWM is recognized by the Carnegie Foundation as both a Research 1 and Community Engaged university. Our mission as an urban research university aligns with this dual designation and is key to our shared, aspirational purposes reflected in our 2030 Strategic Plan to advance student success, the employee experience, research impact and innovation, as well as fiscal sustainability and economic growth. As one of the few Carnegie R1 Research Universities with a Community Engagement designation, UWM offers a unique opportunity to make a significant impact on public health through research , teaching, and collaboration.   Compensation: The target hiring range is $110,000 - $160,000 on a 9-month, academic basis. Final salary offer will be contingent on rank and skills and abilities demonstrated throughout the selection process Required Qualifications: PhD /Doctorate . in social work or a related field.     Ability to meet rank of a tenured associate or full professor in the Department of Social Work at UWM which inclu des a strong record of research and scholarship, teaching, and service accomplishments comparable to those of a tenured associate or full professor .      Proven success in securing external research funding , preferably federal funding in areas of applied AI.   Demonstrated commitment to fostering inclusive environments and supporting the success of students, including historically underrepresented students ( e.g. first-generation students, Pell-eligible students) .   Preferred Qualifications: Proven track record of interdisciplinary research collaboration related to AI and Data Science .   Demonstrated experience in developing and sustaining partnerships with local organizations.   Strong record of community-engaged research that addresses systemic challenges, employs empowered and emancipatory approaches, and generates equitable and inclusive outcomes.   Demonstrated contributions to advancing underrepresented areas in AI, with tangible impacts on societal benefits, ethical AI development, and reducing biases and inequities in technology access.   Expertise in analyzing and addressing systemic challenges affecting urban and underrepresented populations using AI .    How to Apply: Interested candidates are required to submit the following:   Curriculum vitae.   Letter of interest describing research interests, past accomplishments, qualifications, funding record, and vision for collaborative work with Northwestern Mutual Data Science Institute .   Referee List (names and contact information of at least three references). Letters of recommendation will be requested by the search committee after the initial review .   Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach all required documents prior to submitting the online application to ensure full consideration. Additional documents submitted aside from these required materials will not be considered.     In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to ascertain from the candidates’ application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications.   This is a continuous recruitment with an initial review date of October 19, 2026 .  To ensure consideration, applications must be submitted by October 18, 2026. Application materials received after October 18, 2026 may not receive consideration.   Be part of UWM’s mission to transform lives and communities through research, collaboration, and community engagement.   Questions about this position should be directed to Joshua Xiong at jxxiong @uwm.edu. Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Milwaukee/job/Milwaukee-WI/Social-Work-Open-Rank-Faculty_JR10014758": {
      "title": "Social Work Open Rank Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Social Work Open Rank Faculty Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Job Duties: The Department of Social Work at the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee is seeking to fill two full-time tenured or tenure-track, open rank faculty positions. Duties  include advancing a robust program of research while  maintaining  a strong commitment to teaching and mentorship along with service to the department, university, profession, and community.  The standard teaching load is 4 courses per year (2 courses per semester).   The anticipated start date for the 9-month annual appointment is August 23 rd , 2027.   Key Job Responsibilities: Department: The Helen Bader School of Social Welfare is comprised of the Department of Social Work and the Department of Criminal Justice & Criminology. The Department of Criminal Justice & Criminology offers a BS and an MS, as well as jointly offering a PhD in Social Welfare with the Department of Social Work. The Department is known for its commitment to collaborative partnerships with community and governmental organizations in southeast Wisconsin and beyond through research and student engagement.  Compensation: The target hiring salary is a minimum of $88,000 on a 9-month, academic basis. Final salary offer will be contingent upon rank and skills and abilities demonstrated throughout the selection process. Required Qualifications: Qualified applicants must hold a PhD /Doctorate   in social work or a related field. Applicants who are ABD will be considered if completion of the degree is imminent (received on or before the start date of the position).   Preferred Qualifications: MSW degree   Demonstrated ability to produce significant research   Demonstrated ability to successfully teach undergraduate and graduate courses   Demonstrated ability to collaborate across disciplines and with community partners   How to Apply: Applicants   are required to   apply online   via Workday   A completed application will include 1) a letter of interest, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) a professional writing sample, and 4) c ontact information for three professional references .      Note: Applicants must complete all required fields and attach   all   required documents prior to   submitting   the online application to ensure full consideration.   Additional   documents   submitted   aside from   these required materials  will not be considered.    In instances where the Search and Screen Committee is unable to   ascertain   from the candidates’ application materials whether they meet any of the qualifications, they may be evaluated as not meeting such qualifications .   This is a continuous recruitment with  an initial  review date of October 10, 2026 .   To ensure consideration, applications must be  submitted  by October 9, 2026. Application materials received after October 9,   2026  may not   receive consideration .     Questions about the position should be directed to Tanner Neahous tneahous@uwm.edu Reasonable Accommodations Pursuant to university policy , UWM provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities who are employees or applicants for employment. Employment opportunities will not be denied because of the need to make reasonable accommodations for a qualified individual with a disability. If you need assistance or accommodation in applying because of a disability, please contact uwm-jobs@uwm.edu or 414-229-4463. This job announcement and other material on this site will be made available in alternate formats upon request to an individual with a disability. Confidentiality of Applicant Materials UWM is a State agency and subject to Wisconsin’s Open Records Law. UWM will not, however, reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing except as may be required by Wisconsin’s Open Records law. In certain circumstances, the identities of “final candidates” and/or the identity of the appointed applicant must be revealed upon request. CBC and Reference Check Policy Employment will require a criminal background check. UWM takes the prevention of sexual misconduct seriously. As such, you and your references will be required to answer questions regarding any past findings of sexual violence and sexual harassment or any pending allegations of sexual violence or sexual harassment. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report (Clery Act) For the UWM Annual Security and Fire Safety Reports (i.e., Clery Report), which includes statistics about reported crimes and information about campus security policies, see  http://www.cleryact.uwm.edu/ , or call UWM’s Dean of Students Office, Student Union 345 at (414) 229-4632 for a paper copy. Employment Authorization In compliance with federal law, all persons at the time of their start date will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification form upon hire. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
